{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794181548,"nonce":"6TMCkHTEfKS+5qp6BAs/5hhZOI0=","title":"The description open in an editor follows the tracker, and a write never overwrites an edit it did not see","message":"When the description is open in git's editor (`esc e`) or in show's description box, and someone else changes it in the tracker (another process, a pull, a Jira sync), the person editing finds out in their editor, and their write never silently overwrites the other edit.\n\n## Design\n\nAspect that changes: `doc/design/views.md` (\"The external editor\", \"Editing the description\"). Scope: the description only; the comment box writes new comments, and nothing in the terminal edits an existing comment.\n\n1. **The file is live while the editor is open.** While `esc e` has the description open, a watcher owned by the editor hand-off (a goroutine registered as an observer on the issues subcache; the page's event loop is blocked while the editor runs) re-reads the issue on each event and, when the description's `message_hash` differs from the text last put in the file, rewrites the file. The write goes through a temporary file renamed over the path, so the editor never reads a half-written file. The watcher stops when the editor exits. The editor's own changed-on-disk handling (vim's \"changed since reading it\", emacs, nano, VS Code's compare) is the conflict UI.\n2. **What is written is `git merge-file`**: base = the tracker text last put in the file, ours = the file as it is on disk, theirs = the new tracker text. A file the person has not saved over is the base, so it becomes the new tracker text exactly; a file they have saved is merged, non-overlapping edits cleanly and overlaps with conflict markers, so nothing they saved is lost even when an editor reloads a clean buffer silently. Run through git (an exec, as `gitcli` does for what go-git lacks).\n3. **An untouched write is reverted.** If the editor exits with the file still exactly what git-work last wrote into it (a refresh or a merge), the person left nothing of their own: the draft is the tracker's current text, as if the merge had not been done, and there is nothing to write.\n4. **The edit names its base.** The base is the hash of the tracker text the file was last given (doc/design/issue.md, \"An edit can name its base\"). `Enter` after the editor's exit writes with that base; a person who saved over the refreshed file did so after their editor warned them, and their text wins.\n5. **The description box carries a base too.** The box records the description's hash when it opens (and when the editor's text comes back, per 4). `Enter` on a stale base is refused, the draft kept, the status saying the description changed since it was opened; `esc e` then opens the editor on the `git merge-file` of the box's base, its draft and the current text, with that text's hash as the new base, and rule 3 applies to it.\n\nApproved by the user in a Claude Code session on 2026-11-08: they chose the merge (with item 3, which is their addition) and the box in the same story.","files":null,"fields":{"area":["tui"],"status":"in-progress","type":"story"}}]}